--TEST--
`$uri->path` ends at the first `?` or `#` and at no other byte: an escaped one is a byte inside the path, a `/` or a `?` after them belongs to the query or the fragment, and over a corpus of relative references the three components rebuild the whole text exactly — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 3.3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring `uri-path-is-the-one-component-that-always-exists` case
// counts where the path is *absent* — nowhere. This one asks the other
// question a component has: where it ends. RFC 3986 § 3.3 gives the path no
// terminator of its own; it is whatever precedes the first `?` or `#`, and
// those two bytes are delimiters only in the position they appear in. So the
// claim is a partition, and it is asserted by rebuilding the text rather than
// by reading three answers off a line.

final class Tail {
    // The whole of a reference that has neither a scheme nor an authority,
    // rebuilt out of the three readers that can see it. `null` and `""` are
    // different for the last two — `?` with nothing after it is an empty
    // query — so the delimiter is written from the absence, never from the
    // length.
    public static function of(string $written): string {
        var $uri = Core\Uri::parse($written);
        string $out = $uri->path();
        ?string $query = $uri->query();
        if ($query != null) {
            $out = $out . "?" . $query;
        }
        ?string $fragment = $uri->fragment();
        if ($fragment != null) {
            $out = $out . "#" . $fragment;
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// Relative references, so that the text and the tail are the same string and
// the rebuild has nothing else to account for. Every shape the two delimiters
// can take: absent, present and empty, present with the other delimiter's byte
// inside it, and both escaped.
array<string> $corpus = [
    "a/b",
    "a/b?q",
    "a/b#f",
    "a/b?q#f",
    "a/b?",
    "a/b#",
    "a/b?#",
    "a/b?q/r#f/g",
    "a/b?q#f?g",
    "a%3Fb",
    "a%23b",
    "",
    "?q",
    "#f",
];
int $rebuilt = 0;
foreach ($corpus as string $written) {
    if (Tail::of($written) == $written) { $rebuilt = $rebuilt + 1; }
}
echo "rebuilt from three readers ", $rebuilt, " of ", Core\Arr::count($corpus) as int, "\n";

// The rows the count is made of, so a `14` above is readable: `-` is absent,
// `[…]` is present and possibly empty. The three interesting ones are the
// last three, and each is a bound named against the row above it.
final class Show {
    public static function some(?string $held): string {
        if ($held == null) {
            return "-";
        }
        return "[" . $held . "]";
    }
}
foreach ($corpus as string $row) {
    var $each = Core\Uri::parse($row);
    echo "[", $row, "] path [", $each->path(), "] query ", Show::some($each->query()),
         " fragment ", Show::some($each->fragment()), "\n";
}

// The three bounds, said in one line each rather than left to be read out of
// the table. A `?` is a delimiter only *before* the first `#`, so the one in
// `?q#f?g` is the query's and the one after the `#` is a byte of the fragment.
// A `/` after either delimiter is likewise an ordinary byte, which is what
// stops a query from looking like more path. And an *escaped* `?` or `#` is
// not a delimiter at all, which is the whole reason `Core\Uri::encodeComponent`
// escapes them: a path segment holding one cannot open a query.
echo "escaped ? [", Core\Uri::parse("a%3Fb")->path(), "] plain ? [",
     Core\Uri::parse("a?b")->path(), "]\n";
echo "escaped # [", Core\Uri::parse("a%23b")->path(), "] plain # [",
     Core\Uri::parse("a#b")->path(), "]\n";

// With an authority in front, the same rule decides where the path *starts*
// as well: the authority ends at the first `/`, `?` or `#`, so a reference
// with a query and no path has the empty path rather than a path of `?q`.
echo "authority then query [", Core\Uri::parse("http://h?q")->path(),
     "] then fragment [", Core\Uri::parse("http://h#f")->path(), "]\n";
--EXPECT--
rebuilt from three readers 14 of 14
[a/b] path [a/b] query - fragment -
[a/b?q] path [a/b] query [q] fragment -
[a/b#f] path [a/b] query - fragment [f]
[a/b?q#f] path [a/b] query [q] fragment [f]
[a/b?] path [a/b] query [] fragment -
[a/b#] path [a/b] query - fragment []
[a/b?#] path [a/b] query [] fragment []
[a/b?q/r#f/g] path [a/b] query [q/r] fragment [f/g]
[a/b?q#f?g] path [a/b] query [q] fragment [f?g]
[a%3Fb] path [a%3Fb] query - fragment -
[a%23b] path [a%23b] query - fragment -
[] path [] query - fragment -
[?q] path [] query [q] fragment -
[#f] path [] query - fragment [f]
escaped ? [a%3Fb] plain ? [a]
escaped # [a%23b] plain # [a]
authority then query [] then fragment []
